"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8835],{28835:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"02-kto-platit","title":"Lesson 02. Who Pays You: Where Real Yield Comes From","sourceFingerprint":"cf60b0f536f66268a7d6d2752dbb085ba5eea839594a79a35b06403c1a8f74fd","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"**Chapter 2. The Source of Profit: Anatomy of Cash Flow**  \\n*Thoughtful reading time: ~7 minutes*"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"> \\"If you are sitting at a card table and haven\'t figured out who is paying for everyone within the first half-hour, it\'s most likely you. The same rule applies in the world of capital: until you determine whose pocket the profit is specifically coming from, your savings are working for someone else\'s financial gain.\\""},{"id":"b003","markdown":"## Laws of the Physics of Capital: Where Money Comes From"},{"id":"b004","markdown":"In business, any viable project begins with a single question: *who exactly is transferring money to the checking account, and what problem are we solving for them?* Without this understanding, it makes no sense to write code, hire people, or open an office."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"In investing, investors surprisingly often forget this basic logic. It seems as if returns are generated by charts in a mobile app or by broker magic. However, return is not a natural phenomenon. It is entirely tangible rubles, yuan, or dollars moving from someone else\'s pocket into yours."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"If the origin of this cash flow cannot be soberly audited, you are buying a \\"pig in a poke.\\" This means you won\'t be able to foresee when the stream will dry up."},{"id":"b007","markdown":"In the real economy, there are only **four legitimate counterparties** ready to transfer their financial resources to you."},{"id":"b008","markdown":"```\\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\\n│                     FOUR SOURCES OF RETURN                      │\\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\\n                               │\\n       ┌───────────────────────┼───────────────────────┐\\n       ▼                       ▼                       ▼                       ▼\\n 1. BORROWER             2. BUSINESS             3. TENANT               4. BUYER\\n (Principal repayment    (Dividend profit +      (Payment for            (Price difference\\n  + interest)             share growth)           space usage)            on resale)\\n```"},{"id":"b009","markdown":"## Breakdown 1. Debtor (Borrower)"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"**Core mechanics:** You temporarily transfer surplus liquidity to a counterparty against an IOU. In return, they repay the original principal along with a pre-agreed reward."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"* **Traditional deposit.** The borrower is a credit institution. The cost of money for a commercial bank is dictated by the regulator — the CBR key rate. It is unprofitable for a bank to borrow from the public at rates significantly higher than the interbank market. Offering rates substantially below the market average means losing depositors to competitors. Therefore, deposit rates strictly track the Central Bank\'s direction.\\n* **Bond issuances.** You lend money to the government (Federal Loan Bonds — OFZ), a municipality/region, or a commercial entity. The debtor commits to paying regular coupons and redeeming the face value on a fixed date. Bonds do not have state deposit insurance (ASV), and the market price of the principal fluctuates every trading day. For this uncertainty, the investor receives a premium: the yield on high-quality corporate bonds traditionally exceeds that of a typical deposit."},{"id":"b012","markdown":"> **When the flow dries up:** In the event of a default (borrower\'s bankruptcy). Within its sovereign currency, the state carries a near-zero risk of non-payment. Blue-chip corporations are reliable. The higher the paper\'s interest rate, the more desperately the issuer needs liquidity, and the higher the probability that they will fail to settle their liabilities."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"## Breakdown 2. Operating Business"},{"id":"b014","markdown":"**Core mechanics:** By purchasing a share, you buy a micro-stake in a real commercial enterprise. Here, the company itself pays you — strictly from earned net profit."},{"id":"b015","markdown":"* **Two paths of cash flow:** Management has the right to distribute a portion of free cash directly to co-owners (dividend yield) or direct it toward building new plants, logistics, and acquiring competitors. If the company\'s investments are effective, the business scales up, market capitalization increases, and your stake appreciates on the exchange.\\n* **Who is at the helm:** All payouts depend on the verdict of the board of directors and the vote at the general meeting of shareholders. A vivid historical lesson: in the summer of 2022, the market expected colossal dividends from Gazprom; however, shareholders voted against the payout. The share price plummeted by 30% in just one trading session. A retail private investor cannot force the board of directors to share profits."},{"id":"b016","markdown":"> **When the flow dries up:** When an enterprise faces a sales crisis, sanctions pressure, increasing fiscal burden, or incompetent management. Debt instruments are obligated to pay under contract; stocks do not guarantee a single penny. The high equity premium over long horizons is the compensation for voluntarily sharing both boom and lean years with the entrepreneur."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"## Breakdown 3. Real Estate Tenant"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"**Core mechanics:** You own physical square meters (an apartment, commercial space, parking space) and lease them for temporary use in exchange for regular payments."},{"id":"b019","markdown":"Many consider residential real estate a passive asset. However, the historical appreciation of per-square-meter prices on the secondary market over the past two decades has merely kept pace with inflation rates. The primary capital growth here is driven precisely by rental checks."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"The nominal rental yield in metropolitan areas hovers around ~6% per annum of the property\'s market value. But \\"gross\\" income differs radically from net profit."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Back-of-the-envelope calculation: honest yield of square meters"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"Let\'s de-anonymize the expenses of a typical one-room apartment worth **10 000 000 ₽**."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"| Income and Expense Item | Calculation for the Year | Balance |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Base incoming flow** (rent 50 000 ₽ / mo) | $50\\\\,000 \\\\times 12$ | $+600\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ (6.0% \\"gross\\") |\\n| **Tax on professional income (self-employment 4%)** | $600\\\\,000 \\\\times 0{,}04$ | $-24\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ |\\n| **Vacancy period between tenants (1 month)** | Lost rent | $-50\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ |\\n| **Utilities and operational expenses during vacancy month** | Bill payments | $-6\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ |\\n| **Depreciation reserve for cosmetic renovations** | Refresh walls, furniture, appliances every 4 years | $-60\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ |\\n| **Minor force majeure** (plumbing, locks, insurance) | Insurance policy + handyman | $-20\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ |\\n| **FINAL NET INCOME** | **Actual cash in hand** | **$+440\\\\,000\\\\ ₽$ (4.4% per annum)** |"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"*Conclusion:* Net rental yield drops to **4.4% per annum**. Meanwhile, the owner spends time on viewings, monitoring payments, and managing household repairs, acting as a micro-property management company."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"## Breakdown 4. The Next Buyer"},{"id":"b026","markdown":"**The essence of the mechanism:** There is an entire class of investment instruments that do not create added value and fundamentally do not generate an incoming cash flow. These include:\\n* Physical gold and tokenized metals (`#PAXG`).\\n* Foreign currency and stablecoins (`USDT`).\\n* Collectible assets: art, vintage cars, rare wristwatches."},{"id":"b027","markdown":"A gold bar in a safe deposit box does not pay dividends. A stack of cash notes does not pay a coupon."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"The only way to lock in a profit on them is **to sell to another market participant for more than you bought it for**. Your income here is entirely funded by *the next buyer*."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"* **Price drivers:** Exclusively the balance of supply and demand in the external market. Gold shoots up in price when the financial system is in turmoil and global investors seek shelter from devaluations. Currency rises against the ruble if the country\'s balance of payments deteriorates.\\n* **Role in the system:** Such instruments are ideal as safety anchors for a portfolio. They protect capital during large-scale crises, but cannot serve as an autonomous engine of long-term wealth: they lack an internal cash flow generator."},{"id":"b030","markdown":"## Hidden Friction: Who Are You Paying?"},{"id":"b031","markdown":"When calculating investment returns, it is important to remember not only those who give money to you, but also the intermediaries who deduct their fees:"},{"id":"b032","markdown":"1. **Exchange broker:** charges a commission for every purchase or sale of assets.\\n2. **Funds (BPIF / ETF):** deduct a management fee (TER) directly from the net asset value every day, regardless of the annual financial result.\\n3. **Banks and agents:** package simple exchange-traded instruments into complex investment insurance policies or structured notes with hidden costs of up to 3–5% at entry."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"Seemingly modest fees of 1–2% per annum over 15–20 years of compound interest can strip away 20% to 35% of your final capital."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"## 30-Second Express Audit of Any Offer"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"Before transferring capital into any financial instrument, ask the promoter of the idea three direct questions:"},{"id":"b036","markdown":"1. **Who exactly is the ultimate source of the payout?** (A borrower, an operating business, a residential tenant, or a future speculator?)\\n2. **What are the sources of their income?** (If you are being told about \\"innovative schemes\\" and an inflow of funds from new investors, you are looking at a classic pyramid scheme).\\n3. **Under what scenario will the payments guaranteed to stop?** (If the manager assures you that there are no risks at all, and the projected return is notably higher than a reliable deposit, you are being deliberately misled)."}],"terms":[{"id":"emintent","term":"Issuer","definition":"a government or company that issues securities."},{"id":"kupon","term":"Bond coupon","definition":"regular interest payment from the borrower to the security holder."},{"id":"arendnyj-dohod","term":"Net rental income","definition":"rental payments minus vacancy periods, repairs, taxes, and depreciation."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"An investor purchased an OFZ-PD (fixed-coupon government bond). Who is the party paying them the yield in this transaction?","options":["The next trader on the Moscow Exchange who will buy the bond at a higher quote.","The state Ministry of Finance as the borrower, transferring coupons and redeeming the face value from the budget.","The bank through whose mobile app the brokerage account was opened."],"correct":1,"explanation":"By purchasing an OFZ, you are lending to the state treasury. The state acts as the debtor: it has an obligation to pay the agreed coupon and repay the principal upon maturity."},{"id":"q2","q":"Why is the real net yield from renting out an apartment almost always noticeably lower than the nominal rate (e.g., 6% per annum)?","options":["Taxes, vacancy periods between tenants, finishing depreciation, and administrative expenses are inevitably deducted from the incoming cash flow.","The law obliges the owner to give 50% of payments to the building management company.","Inflation completely depreciates nominal rental payments every 6 months."],"correct":1,"explanation":"When calculating the real financial result, an investor must discount rental income for tenant search periods, regular repairs, insurance, tax fees, and replacing worn-out appliances."},{"id":"q3","q":"An investor holds tokenized gold (#PAXG) and USDT stablecoins in their portfolio. How can they generate an investment profit?","options":["The gold issuer and crypto exchanges distribute a quarterly share of their net profit.","Exclusively through the emergence of a buyer willing to purchase these assets at a higher price in the future.","Through a guaranteed government coupon pegged to currency inflation."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Safe-haven assets do not generate operational cash flow (dividends or coupons). Any financial return from them comes down to the speculative difference between the purchase and sale price on the open market."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);